      },
      "rev_n_1_b67ced_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_b67ced_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_b67ced",
        "thought_text": "Consider sorting the robots based on their starting positions and then use dynamic programming to count the number of ways to reach each state where a subset of robots remains.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406851.608553
      },
      "rev_n_2_e29c63_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_e29c63_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_e29c63",
        "thought_text": "Explore the problem using graph theory, where each robot is a node, and there's an edge between two nodes if one robot can activate the other. Count the number of independent sets in this graph.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406851.608596
      },
      "rev_n_3_3297da_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_3297da_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_3297da",
        "thought_text": "Use a recursive approach with memoization to explore all possible sequences of activations and count the unique states where a subset of robots remains.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406851.608623
      },
      "rev_n_4_78b43a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_78b43a_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_78b43a",
        "thought_text": "Re-examine the logic for updating the DP array. The current approach might not correctly account for the recursive activation of robots. Consider using a different approach to track the states more accurately.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-3973779830870679565",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406932.824914
      },
      "rev_n_5_92a3bb_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_92a3bb_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_92a3bb",
        "thought_text": "Investigate the possibility of using a segment tree or a similar data structure to efficiently manage and query the ranges of robots' movements. This could help in accurately counting the number of active robots at any point in time.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-3973779830870679565",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406932.824956
      },
      "rev_n_6_f6f19d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_f6f19d_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_f6f19d",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the problem constraints more closely. Since the number of robots (N) can be up to 200,000, a solution needs to be efficient. Consider whether a combinatorial approach or a graph-based approach could provide a more optimal solution.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-3973779830870679565",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406932.824969
      },
      "rev_n_7_d67bd8_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_d67bd8_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_d67bd8",
        "thought_text": "The current approach uses dynamic programming but incorrectly checks if a robot can be included based on its interval overlap. Instead, we should consider the activation chain formed by overlapping intervals. We need to identify connected components in the graph where each component represents a set of robots that can be activated together.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8797105599786853784",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406970.867646
      },
      "rev_n_8_e2a7a7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_e2a7a7_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_e2a7a7",
        "thought_text": "Consider using a topological sorting approach to handle the activation order of robots. By sorting robots based on their starting positions and then processing them in order, we can determine which robots can be activated without causing overlaps. This might help in correctly identifying the independent sets of robots.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8797105599786853784",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406970.867661
      },
      "rev_n_9_8d2bf1_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_8d2bf1_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_8d2bf1",
        "thought_text": "Implement a union-find (disjoint-set) data structure to efficiently manage and merge groups of robots that can activate each other. Each group represents a potential set of robots that can remain on the number line. The number of unique groups will give us the number of possible sets of robots remaining.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8797105599786853784",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794406970.867667
      }
    }
